From: linux-omarchy-aarch64
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 10:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: hamoa: use DRIPS only during system suspend

7.3 already describes domain_ss3 (PSCI 0x0200c354), the system DRIPS
state. Entering it from runtime idle hangs the SoC after the first
s2idle. Windows vetoes it while the screen is on and uses the shallower
platform.SS1 state (ACPI \\_SB.SYSM._LPI, 0x02000154) instead.

Add domain_ss1 for runtime idle and mark domain_ss3 with system-state,
so the genpd governors skip DRIPS until system suspend. purwa.dtsi
includes hamoa.dtsi, so the X1P42100 Vivobook S 15 gets the same states.
The latencies and the SS1 state are the ones used on this laptop in
justririll/x1e-sleep; the property name is Maulik Shah's system-state,
not that tree's system-sleep-only.

Link: https://github.com/justririll/x1e-sleep/blob/4e84b60eb0/kernel/sleep/dts-hamoa-add-ss1-idle-state.patch
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20261113-s2idle_state-v2-0-ad604182e7e7@oss.qualcomm.com/
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
@@ -305,12 +305,23 @@
 				min-residency-us = <7000>;
 			};
 
+			/* ACPI \\_SB.SYSM _LPI platform.SS1 */
+			domain_ss1: domain-sleep-1 {
+				compatible = "domain-idle-state";
+				arm,psci-suspend-param = <0x02000154>;
+				entry-latency-us = <250>;
+				exit-latency-us = <250>;
+				min-residency-us = <7500>;
+			};
+
 			domain_ss3: domain-sleep-0 {
 				compatible = "domain-idle-state";
 				arm,psci-suspend-param = <0x0200c354>;
 				entry-latency-us = <2500>;
 				exit-latency-us = <2500>;
 				min-residency-us = <9000>;
+				/* DRIPS from runtime idle hangs the SoC after the first s2idle */
+				system-state;
 			};
 		};
 	};
@@ -470,7 +481,7 @@
 
 		system_pd: power-domain-system {
 			#power-domain-cells = <0>;
-			domain-idle-states = <&domain_ss3>;
+			domain-idle-states = <&domain_ss1>, <&domain_ss3>;
 		};
 	};
 
